#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbe35c7b-8068-5b28-a820-d25e6b62565a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots Using the Quadratic Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "2e80a8b3-de45-51f0-9241-a699222da37b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4157" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\, x^2-4x+5=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1, b=-4,$ and $c=5.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16-20}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=2+\\textrm{i}$ and $x=2-\\textrm{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c8c1d8-2662-5c15-b084-084f9e930141" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "af6ecee9-30de-5913-be21-f184d6f9cacd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4160" :question/problem "The equation $x^2+8x+80=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^2=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x+80 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x & =-80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x & =-80 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =-80 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}-16 & =-80 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4,$ $q=-64,$ and $p+q = -60.$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =-64 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-64} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 8\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-4+8\\,\\textrm{i}$ and $x=-4-8\\,\\textrm{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087632 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef8dec7d-5154-59ee-aee8-9f068eb6b8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging and Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "7f7e9924-1c7b-516c-928c-962a4cd5cbcc" :question/math-academy-id "e-7517" :question/problem "Find the solutions of the equation $4x^2= 4x-13 .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 4x + 13=0.\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are  $a=4, b=-4,$ and $c=13.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(4)(13)}}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16-208}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-192}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 8\\sqrt{-3}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 8\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}+ \\sqrt{3} \\, \\textrm{i}$ and $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}- \\sqrt{3} \\, \\textrm{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef8dec7d-5154-59ee-aee8-9f068eb6b8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging and Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bdb7b3f-b4e5-511c-a044-8968678b8052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bdb7b3f-b4e5-511c-a044-8968678b8052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232361} :answer-field/id #uuid "e898c7ec-99b4-56ec-91de-29be2252ba98" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232360} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0836784b-9a67-52bb-b67c-5452ba0317b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0836784b-9a67-52bb-b67c-5452ba0317b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232363} :answer-field/id #uuid "04b5d2dc-1588-56be-bf8e-3aeebd68c7d2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "118176bc-6145-5e92-99d8-81ec478422c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-250562" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${x}^{2}+22(x+1)=-100.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+22(x+1) & =-100 \\\\ {x}^{2}+22x+22+100 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+22x+122 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1,\\,b=22,$ and $c=122.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-22\\pm \\sqrt{(22)^{2}-4(1)(122)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-22\\pm \\sqrt{484-488}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-22\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-22\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-11\\pm 1\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-11+\\text{i}$ and $x=-11-\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef8dec7d-5154-59ee-aee8-9f068eb6b8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging and Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a54d4b3-517d-57b6-846c-05a3d76fe1e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522039} {:answer/id #uuid "14d76f83-3406-5a16-9d41-9302c71facc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\pm 6\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522062} {:answer/id #uuid "33b525d2-ae01-5ef2-9eed-e166e957cf3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522133} {:answer/id #uuid "85e82733-72e9-5fe3-a525-fcd438f48a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522287} {:answer/id #uuid "d1db860b-027a-52ce-b830-0bd7940d1d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33b525d2-ae01-5ef2-9eed-e166e957cf3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522133} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb786784-ac71-5285-99ae-753bd9071aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8883de5f-4ad3-53e1-a8c2-678c96063be9" :question/math-academy-id "q-28178" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\,-6x=-{x}^{2}-13.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+13=0.\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1,\\,b=-6,$ and $c=13.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-6)\\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^{2}-4(1)(13)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{36-52}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=3+2\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=3-2\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbe35c7b-8068-5b28-a820-d25e6b62565a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots Using the Quadratic Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a7e83d3-fd36-54f6-8c7e-359e5342900d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522301} {:answer/id #uuid "b13fd961-7770-597d-9894-1aaa35b12ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522384} {:answer/id #uuid "bb86bfcb-bc62-5616-a522-004bcf66c2a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522407} {:answer/id #uuid "d913210a-0e0c-54c6-9454-1a19c74216c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522470} {:answer/id #uuid "e8544b0b-372f-5c72-87c3-02d17f08022a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a7e83d3-fd36-54f6-8c7e-359e5342900d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522301} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aee3ac9-76aa-5429-a6bd-558c79881bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bb5e35b-a0ec-5519-8ca8-cd918f5d6df4" :question/math-academy-id "q-54641" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-{x}^{2}+2x=5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to make the right-hand side equal to zero:\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+2x-5=0\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=-1,b=2,$ and $c=-5.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4(-1)(-5)}}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-20}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}}{-2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=1+2\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=1-2\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbe35c7b-8068-5b28-a820-d25e6b62565a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots Using the Quadratic Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7a1cb3-fb66-5b84-aa5f-f8b993b9ee24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522048} {:answer/id #uuid "496972a5-0381-57a0-944b-a695d886f0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522173} {:answer/id #uuid "59f8db65-0a6c-55f8-924a-17143dd00241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522205} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb70bb5-fdce-54d6-b98c-a532459a6a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522310} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8ff6a6-1bd6-5c26-a137-c745cab890a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496972a5-0381-57a0-944b-a695d886f0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522173} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f985998-b06f-5ab1-b71e-02977fd6c7e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f34b8912-4b56-5d41-974a-bbda413ccbb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-54642" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-2{x}^{2}+4x-8=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=-2,b=4,$ and $c=-8.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4(-2)(-8)}}{2(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{16-64}}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-48}}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\sqrt{-3}}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}{-4} \\\\  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c8c1d8-2662-5c15-b084-084f9e930141" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3133f8fb-535f-5f80-a6b0-05a8e94cab76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-4,\\,x=-1\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522126} {:answer/id #uuid "61dc17df-7f2c-5e9a-8fae-4b878df5b615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-2,\\,x=-1\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522223} {:answer/id #uuid "7393a346-1d74-5db6-8784-b142b1da6218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-2,\\,x=-2\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522248} {:answer/id #uuid "a84c129c-dae5-55d4-9722-06177a4516ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-6,\\,x=-2\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522374} {:answer/id #uuid "c208198d-363c-5ae6-b1e5-01493c975478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-2,\\,x=-2\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c208198d-363c-5ae6-b1e5-01493c975478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-2,\\,x=-2\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522424} :answer-field/id #uuid "463f4433-cc91-5575-9df5-d220fc7f2416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36266739-216f-533b-a7c0-f932ac7e366b" :question/math-academy-id "q-66233" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}+4x+8=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x & =-8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =-8 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-4 & =-8 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2,\\,q=-4,$ and $p+q=-2.$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-4} \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm 2\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =-2\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-2+2\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-2-2\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbe35c7b-8068-5b28-a820-d25e6b62565a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots Using the Quadratic Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b189539-dd25-545f-a331-7dbf880d4d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522081} {:answer/id #uuid "32b8521f-bc62-5cd2-a472-16a8d177d2fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522131} {:answer/id #uuid "7e79f5b9-a947-5b53-a7b6-fd87e3a8c7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522268} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ce87d9-15e2-556f-9f82-2ec6781b54be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522510} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ac98b0-f615-5f5c-91a6-dc79abe795a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e79f5b9-a947-5b53-a7b6-fd87e3a8c7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522268} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c483242-14ab-518f-a252-0e040b788c54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc9d445a-7899-5437-a552-e9338bc6c692" :question/math-academy-id "q-66426" :question/problem "Solve  the equation $\\,-{x}^{2}-8x-41=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=-1,b=-8,$ and $c=-41.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-8)\\pm \\sqrt{(-8)^{2}-4(-1)(-41)}}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{64-164}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}}{-2} \\\\  & =-4\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-4+5\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-4-5\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c8c1d8-2662-5c15-b084-084f9e930141" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e561ad-263f-5e12-967a-1f08037c6fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-50,\\,x=2\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522017} {:answer/id #uuid "197bab39-aefb-5013-8acb-1aa1f1a8cb64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-48,\\,x=1\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522076} {:answer/id #uuid "3157fdc1-ded2-5a92-9105-4346fef8e904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-50,\\,x=1\\pm 7\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522127} {:answer/id #uuid "add29e6d-84a5-5adf-8899-1715f944a39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-48,\\,x=2\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522381} {:answer/id #uuid "e6426f70-ad93-5d31-97ad-d5c4192bec98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-50,\\,x=1\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3157fdc1-ded2-5a92-9105-4346fef8e904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-50,\\,x=1\\pm 7\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522127} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c344069-8f49-5427-9186-66d3592936f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe69821d-b46b-58da-90bc-7fc6e8ec74de" :question/math-academy-id "q-66433" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-2x+50=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+50 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =-50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x & =-50 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =-50 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1 & =-50 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1,\\,q=-49,$ and $p+q=-50.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =-49 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-49} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 7\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm 7\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=1+7\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=1-7\\,\\text{i}.$"}]}
